    summary: "While manually analyzing over three miles of paper chart records from a giant radio telescope she helped construct, graduate student Jocelyn Bell spotted a tiny 'bit of scruff' that repeated every 1.33 seconds—discovering the first pulsar and proving the existence of spinning neutron stars.",
    content: "In 1965, 22-year-old Northern Irish postgraduate student Jocelyn Bell arrived at the Cavendish Laboratory at the University of Cambridge. For two exhausting years, wearing heavy boots and working through rain and mud, Bell helped build a colossal radio telescope covering four acres of farmland, stringing over 120 miles of wire and copper cable.\n\nWhen the telescope went live in July 1967, it produced vast amounts of data recorded on three-pen paper strip charts. Every day, rolls of chart paper cascaded across the floor. Without computer analysis, Bell examined hundreds of feet of ink traces daily with a hand lens.\n\nIn late autumn, she noticed a faint, irregular smudge occupying less than a quarter-inch of paper—what she called a \"bit of scruff.\" Most people would have dismissed it as random engine ignition noise from passing trucks. But Bell recognized that this particular scruff appeared at the exact same sidereal time each night, fixed among the stars!\n\nShe adjusted the high-speed paper feed. On November 28, 1967, the signal resolved into an astonishing sequence of razor-sharp pulses ticking every 1.337 seconds with absolute precision—far more accurate than any mechanical clock on Earth!\n\nHer supervisor, Antony Hewish, initially thought it was human interference or radar. When all Earthly origins were ruled out, they half-jokingly nicknamed the mystery beacon **LGM-1** for 'Little Green Men', wondering if it could be an extraterrestrial beacon.\n\nSoon, Bell located a second pulsating signal in a completely different part of the sky, pulsing every 1.2 seconds. It was not aliens; it was a natural cosmic powerhouse!\n\nShe had discovered a **pulsar**—a superdense neutron star, the crushed remnant of an exploded giant sun, packing the mass of our entire Sun into a ball just twelve miles across, spinning at breakneck speed.\n\nIn 1974, the Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded for the discovery of pulsars—scandalously given to her supervisor Antony Hewish and Martin Ryle, bypassing Bell. Yet history has given Jocelyn Bell Burnell her rightful place as one of the sharpest, most dedicated observers in the annals of science.",
